**Day 29: Mini Project – Student Records DB **  

**Topic:**  
Designing and implementing a fully normalized Library Database using MySQL. The project involves applying ER modeling, normalization principles, and SQL skills to create a functional database for managing books, members, book issues, and returns.  

**Learning Objectives:**  
• Apply ER design principles to model a Library DB.  
• Implement normalization (1NF, 2NF, 3NF) to eliminate redundancy.  
• Write SQL queries for CRUD operations on library entities.  
• Integrate MySQL Workbench and Draw.io for design and implementation.  

**Activities / Deliverables:**  
• Design an ER diagram for the Library DB using Draw.io.  
• Create normalized tables (books, members, issues, returns) in MySQL.  
• Write SQL scripts for data insertion, updates, and retrieval.  
• Validate database integrity with sample queries.  

**Important Concepts:**  
• Entity-Relationship (ER) modeling for database design.  
• Normalization (1NF, 2NF, 3NF) to ensure data consistency.  
• Foreign key constraints for relational integrity.  

**SQL Syntax / Commands:**  
• `CREATE TABLE` with primary/foreign keys.  
• `INSERT INTO` for populating tables.  
• `JOIN` queries to retrieve related data (e.g., books issued by a member).  

**Real-world Use Cases:**  
• Tracking book inventory and member transactions in libraries.  
• Automating overdue fine calculations for returned books.  

**Tools / Tech:**  
• MySQL 8.0  
• MySQL Workbench  
• Draw.io (for ER diagrams)  

**Summary:**  
Today’s mini-project consolidated ER design, normalization, and SQL skills by building a Library DB. The hands-on exercise emphasized real-world database development, from diagramming to query execution.  

